// road has been tried); the cut-scene Casting Off; the deck (#26 Etekō, #27 Don't Lift The Lid) and its compass close-up;
// the Uttermost Parts' edits (the river mouth, the lighter, Gonroku's goodbye); Osaka (#28 The Dōjima Shore, #29 What The
// Road God Said, #30 Berlin Blue, #31 Three To A Puppet); the cut-scene The Kyoto Boat; and Yui's history pass (the relay
// courier and the komusō in its street). WRITE-21B writes the other column (edo-d.en.js); the phase-B lead joins them.
//   "line.id": ["who", "What they say."]
// Scene shorts: village and shop (additions only: round twenty's lines are edo-a.en.js), kurasawa, satta, castoff, deck,
// compass, dojima, street, theatre, boat. A look is edo.<short>.<thing>.<who>, a use edo.<short>.<thing>.use.<who>, a use
// with a carried thing edo.<short>.<thing>.with.<item>.<who>; a look that changes with the story has a state word before
// the who (edo.satta.fuji.clear.mom). The talks are trees (js/content/talk/edo-c.js); an option is a line here, said by the
// one who picks it. S/work-21-writeA/ids.md says when every line is said, and which hooks and facts the trees use.
//
// THE RULES OF THE ACT, as these lines keep them:
//   - Nobody in the family lies, all act. Every option the player can pick for them is true; the comedy is in exactly true
//     answers ("We're IN a pass."; "They're at work."). They never answer a magistrate's man: someone who has made it true
//     answers for them (O-Natsu: "My helpers.").
//   - Japanese words are few, of the period, and each glossed once by its speaker or by Big Sister: "tegata" (O-Kuni:
//     edo.kuni.pass.2), "saru" (Kōta's slip, edo.kota.monkey.1; Denzō says what it sounds like, edo.deck.freeze.4), "etekō"
//     (Kōta, edo.kota.monkey.2 or edo.kota.word.lil.2), "tsuge" (Big Sister, edo.street.comb.3), "yomiuri" (the sellers
//     themselves, edo.street.chant.1). Round twenty's (arigatō, Funadama, okage-sama de) as they were; "mon" as round twenty
//     used it. No "itadakimasu", no "gochisōsama", no "kawaraban", no "Bunraku"; nobody remarks that everyone understands
//     everyone (Kōta's game with Little Sister is charades, not a lesson in words).
//   - WRITE-25 (round twenty-five): the ship first (Casting Off, the deck, the compass, the Uttermost Parts' lines), then the
//     rest of the act (WRITE-25B), written to docs/WRITING.md, which wins over these rules: the narrator answers looks and
//     tries; boxes run to 115 characters spoken and 150 narrated; capitals and "!" are rationed by number.
//   - Big Sister and Mom state only what the fact files confirm (S/work-21-factsA to -factsD, and round twenty's two); the
//     Japanese people say what the same files say of their own time. docs/CHARACTERS.md's facts rows give each of Big
//     Sister's and Mom's facts with its item; S/work-21-writeA/ready.md lists the others' by fact-file section, and every
//     fact wanted and not used. Inventions are the design's (section 2, "What the game invents") and ready.md's few.
//   - Scripture: Jeremiah 31:17, King James Version exactly, as Mom's own quiet prayer at the Osaka crossroads, in two boxes
//     that together are the verse letter for letter (edo.street.pray.1, .2); Big Sister says the reference (.3), as only
//     she may. Little Sister's grace at O-Natsu's steps is her own words.
//   - Nothing frightening: the yakko penalty is said once, gently, by O-Shige, and Mom's answer ends it; the Kumano-nada is
//     talked about, never shown; the magistrate's men are tired (Kurata) and sentimental (Tatsu); the broadsheet is a
//     strange story, and nobody is chased but two men with a sheaf of paper, who are quicker.
//   - No song, hymn or poem quoted: the puppet play and the chanter's cues are the game's own words; the food boats' call is
//     the documented one, in English; nobody sings.
//   - The round-nineteen box, at WRITING.md's lengths since round twenty-five: one idea, the punch word last,
//     contractions, few exclamation marks, one capital word a box at most.

